# The build context is what `docker build` uploads before a single line of the Dockerfile runs,
# so everything excluded here is time saved on every build and a layer that cannot invalidate the
# cache. `COPY . .` in the builder is deliberate - `cargo build` needs the whole workspace - which
# is exactly why this file has to name the things it does not need.
#
# Measured on a checkout of this repository: `target/` alone was 47.7 GB, and `.git/` 49.6 MB. Both
# were being sent to the daemon and written into a layer *before* the compiler started.

# Build output. The image builds its own, and a host `target/` is the wrong architecture, the
# wrong profile, or both — on Windows it also contains `.pdb` files nothing in a Linux image reads.
target/

# Version control. `COPY . .` does not need the history; the release workflow checks out before it
# builds, so nothing in the Dockerfile reads a git ref.
.git/
.github/

# Documentation and packaging are not compiled. The exception is `packaging/docker`, which the
# Dockerfile itself is under — but the file is passed with `-f`, not copied.
docs/
packaging/

# Editor and OS noise, which changes constantly and would invalidate the build layer for a reason
# no compile depends on.
.idea/
.vscode/
.vscode-test/
*.swp
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Local scratch output. `temp/` is in `.gitignore` for the same reason: it is a working directory,
# not an input.
temp/

# Anything a local `.cargo/config.toml` set up for the host machine. The image's toolchain is
# declared by the base image tag, so a host override that pointed `rustc` somewhere else would
# produce a binary the Dockerfile's comments do not describe.
.cargo/config.toml
.cargo/config

# Environment files. A `.env` on a developer's machine names a database and a key; neither belongs
# in an image layer, and a build that depended on one would work only on that machine.
.env
.env.*
